  "narrative_markdown": "## Overview\nSlightly warmer and clearer than yesterday. Dry until evening thunderstorms. Cooling and becoming calmer through Friday, with rain becoming more likely.\n\n## Today's Forecast\nDaytime maximum temperatures reaching 31.2°C / 88°F under clear to partly cloudy morning skies, before convective development brings showers and thunderstorms late afternoon and evening between 16:00 and 20:00. Overnight minimum temperatures will settle near 18.8°C / 66°F. Surface winds start east-northeasterly overnight, turning south-southwest by midday, with calibrated peak gusts reaching 42.1 km/h (23 kt) near convective activity. Solar UV exposure peaks at a very high 9.4 near midday. Air quality is modeled at 77 (Moderate); local ground sensors were last recorded 3 hours ago at Dunga Beach (AQI 62).\n\n## Extended Outlook\nA cooling and moistening trend sets in across the Nyanza Basin over the next three days. On Wednesday (16 September) and Thursday (17 September), afternoon highs drop into the 28°C–30°C / 82°F–86°F range with rain likelihood increasing significantly, peaking on Thursday with widespread rain expected across the basin. Into the 4–7 day period (Friday through Monday), daytime temperatures remain suppressed near 25°C–29°C / 77°F–84°F with intermittent rainfall. At Day+3 and Day+7, model verification shows ECMWF and Best Match maintaining strong skill, while GFS tends to over-predict daytime maximum temperatures.\n\n## Severe Weather / Hazard Potential\nConvective instability is high across the region today, with peak CAPE values building significantly across model guidance, led by UKMO reaching 4410 J/kg, ECMWF at 1750 J/kg, Best Match at 1660 J/kg, and ICON at 1160 J/kg around 19:00 EAT. Strong, sudden convective downdraft gusts exceeding 42 km/h (23 kt) are possible in the vicinity of any thunderstorm late afternoon and evening, regardless of general background wind forecasts. Frequent lightning and localized heavy downpours present additional hazards during storm passage.\n\n## Winam Gulf — Conditions for Boaters\nCalm to light easterly morning breeze across Winam Gulf turning south-southwesterly around midday at 10–15 km/h (5–8 kt). Conditions deteriorate substantially late afternoon and evening as thunderstorms move over the lake from the eastern highlands. Boaters should anticipate sudden severe wind squalls, unpredictable wave chop, and reduced visibility in heavy downpours between 16:00 and 21:00 EAT.\n\n## Detailed Discussion\n### Synoptic Overview\nAcross roughly 2,600 km, pressure is lowest toward the northeast (1008 hPa) and highest toward the southeast (1019 hPa) — a 11 hPa spread, a strong large-scale gradient. Pressure overhead is near-steady. Sampling is a nine-point ring at 12-degree spacing, so this locates a direction, not a centre or a front. Regional MSLP across the Nyanza Basin averages 1013.1 hPa at Kisumu, 1013.4 hPa to the north, 1013.3 hPa to the west, and 1015.6 hPa over the southeastern highlands. Daytime solar heating over land will establish strong lake-breeze convergence along the Nyanza trough, anchoring intense evening storm development.\n\n### Forecaster Confidence Notes\nModel selection is heavily informed by established long-run review findings. At Day+0, Best Match is established as the strongest rain caller in the basin (86% accuracy), whereas GFS is the weakest (63%) and systematically under-forecasts peak wind speeds (+20.3 km/h mean error) and over-forecasts high temperatures (-1.0°C mean error). The Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) local bulletin calls for a maximum of 31.0°C and minimum of 17.0°C with light afternoon showers, which closely aligns with the numerical model consensus and Forecaster's Call (31.2°C / 18.8°C with late afternoon/evening thunderstorms). Local ground AQI stations currently report stale readings from 3 hours ago: Kisumu Airport (AQI 52, PM2.5 52.0 µg/m³), Ochieng' Avenue (AQI 41, PM2.5 41.0 µg/m³), and Dunga Beach (AQI 62, PM2.5 62.0 µg/m³).",
